  "id": "COMPOUND:11982 - PROTEIN:6298",
  "verbalization": "Microcrystalline cellulose is implicated in the biological pathway leading to Cathepsin E through a series of enzymatic interactions. The dTDP-4-dehydrorhamnose 3,5-epimerase, a target of multiple compounds, serves as a central mediator in this relationship. The compound P-Cresol, 2-Amino-3-(3-oxo-1-propen-1-yl)-2-butenedioic acid, and (+)-Verticillol all act as targets of the dTDP-4-dehydrorhamnose 3,5-epimerase, which in turn targets Cathepsin E. This suggests that Microcrystalline cellulose may influence the activity of this enzyme through the enzymatic conversion of dTDP-4-dehydrorhamnose 3,5-epimerase, which is a key player in the pathway leading to Cathepsin E. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Microcrystalline cellulose could modulate Cathepsin E activity, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
